  "summary": "San Jose officials led by Mayor Matt Mahan unveiled new proposed safeguards for the city's Flock ALPR network, including tighter restrictions on accessing law enforcement data via personal devices, expanded audits, and lobbying Sacramento to stiffen penalties for ALPR data misuse. The proposal follows the April firing of an SJPD officer who allegedly shared plate-reader location data used to track a domestic violence accuser, and comes as the city faces two lawsuits over its 516-camera network.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "ALPRs have helped us solve serious crimes, but we need strict limits on how and when data can be accessed, regular audits to make sure our rules are being followed and real consequences when they're not",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "An investigation determined the officer shared location information with his cousin, who used the data to track a woman accusing him of domestic violence",
      "paragraph_idx": 7
    },
    {
      "quote": "They're PR moves. They do not actually comply with the law and they do not actually protect us",
      "paragraph_idx": 14
    }
  ],
